<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Patiala and East Punjab States Union</h1><p class="st-meta">1948 CE – 1956 CE · capital Patiala</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">PEPSU, the union of the Sikh and other princely states of east Punjab, an Indian state from 1948 to 1956 with Patiala as its capital.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1948 eight <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/patiala-state">Patiala State</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nabha-state">Nabha State</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/jind-state">Jind State</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kapurthala-state">Kapurthala State</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/faridkot-state">Faridkot State</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malerkotla">Malerkotla</a>, Nalagarh and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kalsia-state">Kalsia State</a>, were merged into the Patiala and East Punjab States Union, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yadavindra-singh">Yadavindra Singh</a> of Patiala as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajpramukh">Rajpramukh</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>PEPSU became a Part B state in 1950. Its politics were unstable: after the 1952 elections an Akali-led coalition under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gian-singh-rarewala">Gian Singh Rarewala</a> collapsed, and President's rule was imposed in 1953. It had a predominantly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> rural population and fertile irrigated farmland.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/states-reorganisation-act-1956">States Reorganisation Act</a> PEPSU was merged into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> on 1 November 1956. Its lands were later divided between Punjab, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haryana">Haryana</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himachal-pradesh">Himachal Pradesh</a> in 1966.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/patiala-and-east-punjab-states-union.jpg" alt="Patiala and East Punjab States Union" width="320" height="387"><figcaption>Image: India 1951 Bombay.svg: Superbenjamin this file: Furfur · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PEPSU_in_India_(1951).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1948 CE – 1956 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Patiala</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Yadavindra Singh, Gian Singh Rarewala</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sikhism; Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Punjabi</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>PEPSU</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1952&amp;focus=patiala-and-east-punjab-states-union" aria-label="Patiala and East Punjab States Union on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6683 1032 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Patiala and East Punjab States Union lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7003 1288L7018 1386M7003 1288L6986 1319M7003 1288L6968 1330M7003 1288L6946 1291M7003 1288L6975 1325M7003 1288L6936 1315M7003 1288L6967 1320M7003 1288L7008 1330"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7018 1386h0M6986 1319h0M6968 1330h0M6946 1291h0M6975 1325h0M6936 1315h0M6967 1320h0M7008 1330h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7003 1288h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7003 1288h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1952&amp;focus=patiala-and-east-punjab-states-union">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
